So a reasonable rule of thumb is maybe

265
00:10:42,740 --> 00:10:45,500
you want to raise those numbers up by 10%, not 10 percentage

266
00:10:45,500 --> 00:10:48,460
points, but raise them up by 10% to factor in the transfers.

267
00:10:48,460 --> 00:10:50,140
Because also, of course, a lot of times,

268
00:10:50,140 --> 00:10:53,460
transfers lead to taking extra years because somebody's credit

269
00:10:53,460 --> 00:10:54,260
just transferred.

270
00:10:54,260 --> 00:10:55,620
Is that one?

271
00:10:55,620 --> 00:10:56,340
All right.

272
00:10:56,340 --> 00:10:59,740
So the completion probability, a crucial thing to put in.

273
00:10:59,740 --> 00:11:01,380
And it's even more important than it

274
00:11:01,380 --> 00:11:08,260
seems on the surface because of the dreaded sheepskin effect.

275
00:11:08,260 --> 00:11:10,660
A very strong empirical regularity education

276
00:11:10,660 --> 00:11:12,420
that says that most of the payoff,

277
00:11:12,420 --> 00:11:14,460
at least a disproportionate amount of the payoff,

278
00:11:14,460 --> 00:11:16,660
comes from graduation year.

279
00:11:16,660 --> 00:11:18,180
Earlier years don't pay very much.

280
00:11:18,180 --> 00:11:20,340
It's graduation year that really matters.

281
00:11:20,340 --> 00:11:24,260
So in my book, I do a full literature search.

282
00:11:24,260 --> 00:11:26,340
I come up, for high school, the senior year

283
00:11:26,340 --> 00:11:29,740
pays a bit more than the first three years combined.

284
00:11:29,740 --> 00:11:31,500
For undergraduate, the senior year

285
00:11:31,500 --> 00:11:34,260
pays more than double the first three years combined.

286
00:11:34,260 --> 00:11:35,740
And that's all in percentage terms,

287
00:11:35,740 --> 00:11:39,900
so it's even more lopsided than it sounds.

288
00:11:39,900 --> 00:11:43,620
So what this means is that if you don't finish,

289
00:11:43,620 --> 00:11:47,500
college saying, which is highly likely that you won't finish.

290
00:11:47,500 --> 00:11:50,580
You don't finish, then you are losing almost everything.

291
00:11:50,580 --> 00:11:53,100
You're getting almost nothing financially out of it

292
00:11:53,100 --> 00:11:54,940
because so much of the payoff comes

293
00:11:54,940 --> 00:11:56,860
from crossing the finish line.

294
00:11:56,860 --> 00:11:58,780
Now, notice this here is tacitly assuming

295
00:11:58,780 --> 00:12:00,300
there is no sheepskin effect.

296
00:12:00,300 --> 00:12:02,380
So again, the obvious thing is to redo this

297
00:12:02,380 --> 00:12:04,220
where we've got education years, and then we

298
00:12:04,220 --> 00:12:06,060
have dummy variables from the degrees.

299
00:12:06,060 --> 00:12:09,380
And then again, you'll notice, ah, this is actually

300
00:12:09,380 --> 00:12:11,820
a quite small payoff for years.

301
00:12:11,820 --> 00:12:15,420
It's the degrees that really count.

302
00:12:15,420 --> 00:12:17,220
So this is a huge problem.

303
00:12:17,220 --> 00:12:21,260
And again, if you just go and do some very simple math,

304
00:12:21,260 --> 00:12:23,180
it's pretty obvious what's going on.

305
00:12:23,180 --> 00:12:26,300
So again, suppose that you are lending out money.

306
00:12:26,300 --> 00:12:29,580
All the loans are 10% interest, but suppose that half

307
00:12:29,580 --> 00:12:32,540
don't come back at all.

308
00:12:32,540 --> 00:12:34,700
So suppose half the time, the person

309
00:12:34,700 --> 00:12:37,140
just absconds with the money and you get nothing back.

310
00:12:37,140 --> 00:12:40,260
Then what kind of a profit rate do you actually have?

311
00:12:40,260 --> 00:12:46,540
So 0.5 times 1.1 plus 0.5 times 0.

312
00:12:46,540 --> 00:12:48,700
So imagine it's a 50% repayment rate.

313
00:12:48,700 --> 00:12:54,740
In that case, is your business model even sustainable?

314
00:12:54,740 --> 00:12:56,180
No way.

315
00:12:56,180 --> 00:13:00,700
So what you've got is a rate of return of negative 45%

316
00:13:00,700 --> 00:13:02,420
on your investment.

317
00:13:02,420 --> 00:13:04,340
So you suppose there are no rates.

318
00:13:04,340 --> 00:13:06,020
So that can be like, fine, that's crazy.

319
00:13:06,020 --> 00:13:09,420
Or what if we have 95% repayments

320
00:13:09,420 --> 00:13:12,780
and 5% absconding?

321
00:13:12,780 --> 00:13:13,380
You went there.

322
00:13:13,380 --> 00:13:14,780
Now we're talking.

323
00:13:14,780 --> 00:13:19,580
So a 5% rate of default brings your expected rate

324
00:13:19,580 --> 00:13:21,820
of return down from about 10 to 5.

325
00:13:21,820 --> 00:13:24,460
So it halves the rate.

326
00:13:24,460 --> 00:13:27,500
So again, these defaults are not a marginal issue.

327
00:13:27,500 --> 00:13:30,380
They are the prime issue from the point of view

328
00:13:30,380 --> 00:13:33,580
of the individual investment.

329
00:13:33,580 --> 00:13:36,020
This is such an extreme result. I remember years ago,

330
00:13:36,020 --> 00:13:37,740
I was arguing with Alex Tavarock.

331
00:13:37,740 --> 00:13:39,540
And when I told him this, he just said,

332
00:13:39,540 --> 00:13:41,220
legal labor economists cannot be making

333
00:13:41,220 --> 00:13:44,380
such a ridiculous, stupid, blatant error.

334
00:13:44,380 --> 00:13:46,740
And I said, oh, yes, they are.

335
00:13:46,740 --> 00:13:49,420
And he said, oh, come on, come on.

336
00:13:49,420 --> 00:13:53,820
So we agreed to an arbiter, a well-known and mutually

337
00:13:53,820 --> 00:13:56,060
trusted labor economist, Gordon Dahl.

338
00:13:56,060 --> 00:13:58,020
And so we collectively emailed him.

339
00:13:58,020 --> 00:13:59,420
Gordon, we have a dispute.

340
00:13:59,420 --> 00:14:01,540
I'm not going to tell you which side either is on.

341
00:14:01,540 --> 00:14:03,420
But here's a dispute.

342
00:14:03,420 --> 00:14:07,260
Is it true that a standard return to education estimates

343
00:14:07,260 --> 00:14:09,140
ignore completion probability?

344
00:14:09,140 --> 00:14:10,780
He wrote back, yes.

345
00:14:10,780 --> 00:14:12,420
Yes, it is true.

346
00:14:12,420 --> 00:14:15,020
And Alex was like, what?

347
00:14:15,020 --> 00:14:16,420
Alex was awesome.

348
00:14:16,420 --> 00:14:18,100
And he's one of the very few people

349
00:14:18,100 --> 00:14:19,940
I've ever met who gave me odds, because he

350
00:14:19,940 --> 00:14:21,100
said he was very confident.

351
00:14:21,100 --> 00:14:22,820
So he gave me 2 to 1, paid right off.

352
00:14:26,940 --> 00:14:29,020
So in the last five years, there have

353
00:14:29,020 --> 00:14:32,180
been a few papers on the subject getting published.

354
00:14:32,180 --> 00:14:33,700
Before that, there's one by Atanji,

355
00:14:33,700 --> 00:14:35,540
super famous economist in the early 90s,

356
00:14:35,540 --> 00:14:37,380
and he pointed this out.

357
00:14:37,380 --> 00:14:38,820
Now, you may say, what do you mean?

358
00:14:38,820 --> 00:14:40,860
There's lots of stuff on completion probability.

359
00:14:40,860 --> 00:14:42,860
Now, there's lots of stuff on the determinants

360
00:14:42,860 --> 00:14:44,620
of completion probability.

361
00:14:44,620 --> 00:14:46,500
There, there's tons of papers on that.

362
00:14:46,500 --> 00:14:49,260
How can we get completion probability up?

363
00:14:49,260 --> 00:14:50,980
What is completion probability?

364
00:14:50,980 --> 00:14:52,620
But there's almost no papers that

365
00:14:52,620 --> 00:14:54,380
go and take the completion probability

366
00:14:54,380 --> 00:14:58,900
and put it back into the return calculation.

367
00:14:58,900 --> 00:15:01,100
And if you think that labor economists and education

368
00:15:01,100 --> 00:15:05,540
economists have a pro-education bias, as I do,

369
00:15:05,540 --> 00:15:08,500
I think this is the strongest possible way.

370
00:15:08,500 --> 00:15:10,740
If you think this, it's super suspicious.

371
00:15:10,740 --> 00:15:13,100
It's like, well, but the reason you're trying to raise it

372
00:15:13,100 --> 00:15:16,500
is to get the return up, right?

373
00:15:16,500 --> 00:15:20,020
And so shouldn't we go and talk about how it's

374
00:15:20,020 --> 00:15:22,700
affecting the return right now?

375
00:15:22,700 --> 00:15:25,300
And there's some sense that's defeatist to go and say, well,

376
00:15:25,300 --> 00:15:27,140
what if we're going to go and put in the fact

377
00:15:27,140 --> 00:15:28,380
that half of them fail, then it's

378
00:15:28,380 --> 00:15:30,800
a good ingredient, why am I saying almost none of them

379
00:15:30,800 --> 00:15:31,300
should go?

380
00:15:31,300 --> 00:15:33,580
All right, well, isn't that true?

381
00:15:33,580 --> 00:15:36,300
Until you figure out a way to get that completion probability

382
00:15:36,300 --> 00:15:39,980
way up, isn't it true that it's a bad idea?

383
00:15:39,980 --> 00:15:41,500
And again, this is the kind of thing

384
00:15:41,500 --> 00:15:43,980
where economists have so often mocked people

385
00:15:43,980 --> 00:15:46,580
who open up restaurants ignoring the fact that 80% of them

386
00:15:46,580 --> 00:15:49,220
fail the first year.

387
00:15:49,220 --> 00:15:51,260
And some are going to say, well, but what if you

388
00:15:51,260 --> 00:15:52,540
had a really good idea?

389
00:15:52,540 --> 00:15:54,460
All right, yeah, well, if you had a good idea,

390
00:15:54,460 --> 00:15:54,940
that would change things.

391
00:15:54,940 --> 00:15:56,180
But you probably don't have a good idea.

392
00:15:56,180 --> 00:15:57,860
Why do you think you have a good idea?

393
00:15:57,860 --> 00:16:00,220
Isn't that what almost everybody thinks?

394
00:16:00,220 --> 00:16:04,660
So you need to have some sign on top of your own O of confidence,

395
00:16:04,660 --> 00:16:09,060
which almost everybody at an open restaurant has.

396
00:16:09,060 --> 00:16:12,820
So obviously, if you were giving practical advice to someone

397
00:16:12,820 --> 00:16:14,500
thinking of opening a business, you

398
00:16:14,500 --> 00:16:16,660
should totally factor in the odds

399
00:16:16,660 --> 00:16:20,340
that they lose their life savings, right?

400
00:16:20,340 --> 00:16:22,500
If you know someone who's going to open a business,

401
00:16:22,500 --> 00:16:25,340
will you not reach out to them and try to help them?

402
00:16:25,340 --> 00:16:27,060
Will you not use the power of economics

403
00:16:27,060 --> 00:16:28,980
to save your friend's life savings,

404
00:16:28,980 --> 00:16:31,180
or at least let them be warned?

405
00:16:31,180 --> 00:16:34,460
By the way, you realize what the failure rate is here.

406
00:16:34,460 --> 00:16:36,580
And you might say, maybe you should try a business

407
00:16:36,580 --> 00:16:37,660
with a lower failure rate.

408
00:16:37,660 --> 00:16:39,040
Maybe you want to build a business

409
00:16:39,040 --> 00:16:40,460
for a little lower failure rates.

410
00:16:40,460 --> 00:16:42,860
Maybe you should try something like that.

411
00:16:42,860 --> 00:16:48,340
So this is an enormous factor, which is so neglected,

412
00:16:48,340 --> 00:16:52,620
it's almost mind-boggling, but there it is.

413
00:16:52,620 --> 00:16:54,540
And as I mentioned, it's more mind-boggling

414
00:16:54,540 --> 00:16:56,340
when you interact with the Sheepskin Effect

415
00:16:56,340 --> 00:16:58,980
and realize that doing three years of school

416
00:16:58,980 --> 00:17:04,260
and then giving up has almost no payoff, almost no gross payoff.

417
00:17:04,260 --> 00:17:05,860
So if you have almost no gross payoff

418
00:17:05,860 --> 00:17:08,620
and you have three years of net costs,

419
00:17:08,620 --> 00:17:12,700
then you are really in trouble.

420
00:17:12,700 --> 00:17:15,140
Questions about that?

421
00:17:15,140 --> 00:17:19,940
And now we come to a last horrible problem, which

422
00:17:19,940 --> 00:17:27,220
is that this result does not control for preexisting

423
00:17:27,220 --> 00:17:27,820
ability.

424
00:17:27,820 --> 00:17:29,780
And there's at least one preexisting ability

425
00:17:29,780 --> 00:17:32,900
where we have very strong evidence that it's important.

426
00:17:32,900 --> 00:17:34,500
And that is intelligence.

427
00:17:40,140 --> 00:17:42,660
And intelligence is known to be highly correlated

428
00:17:42,660 --> 00:17:47,980
with education with correlation of 0.6, 0.7.

429
00:17:47,980 --> 00:17:48,820
Yes?

430
00:17:48,820 --> 00:17:50,580
So you said that labor terms don't

431
00:17:50,580 --> 00:17:52,100
have any prediction effect?

432
00:17:52,100 --> 00:17:52,600
Yes.

433
00:17:52,600 --> 00:17:54,380
So they also are not taking into account

434
00:17:54,380 --> 00:17:56,100
what Sheepskin Effect does?

435
00:17:56,100 --> 00:17:59,860
So yes, the Sheepskin Effect, while agreed to by virtually

436
00:17:59,860 --> 00:18:01,420
everyone who's ever looked at the data,

437
00:18:01,420 --> 00:18:03,660
is very rarely, very rarely actually referenced

438
00:18:03,660 --> 00:18:04,380
in most articles.

439
00:18:04,380 --> 00:18:06,140
Most articles just like to do it this way.

440
00:18:08,460 --> 00:18:11,820
So that is the strange thing is the effect

441
00:18:11,820 --> 00:18:15,220
is so large and so consistent in virtually every paper I've ever

442
00:18:15,220 --> 00:18:17,900
found in the last 25 years.

443
00:18:17,900 --> 00:18:19,820
See, when I was debating Eric Haineshek,

444
00:18:19,820 --> 00:18:21,420
he did throw me for a curve by saying,

445
00:18:21,420 --> 00:18:23,020
well, I've got a data set where there's

446
00:18:23,020 --> 00:18:24,300
a much of a Sheepskin Effect.

447
00:18:24,300 --> 00:18:26,980
And so anyway, so at least for college,

448
00:18:26,980 --> 00:18:29,140
his data set doesn't actually have years of college.

449
00:18:29,140 --> 00:18:32,060
So his results aren't really comparable.

450
00:18:32,060 --> 00:18:33,940
For high school, his results are just

451
00:18:33,940 --> 00:18:35,740
at the low end of what other data sets say.

452
00:18:35,740 --> 00:18:37,220
So it's not really that disturbing.

453
00:18:37,220 --> 00:18:39,260
But when you're in a debate with someone,

454
00:18:39,260 --> 00:18:40,820
he says, I got some regressions here.

455
00:18:40,820 --> 00:18:41,940
And they say, you're wrong.

456
00:18:45,060 --> 00:18:47,780
Because I can't honestly say too much about it,

457
00:18:47,780 --> 00:18:50,180
other than it goes against everything and everybody else.

458
00:18:50,180 --> 00:18:54,540
But I didn't want to accuse him of making a statistical error.

459
00:18:54,540 --> 00:18:55,900
It seems super unlikely.

460
00:18:59,300 --> 00:19:01,380
So anyway, you may have noticed that people

461
00:19:01,380 --> 00:19:04,540
that get more education not only are smarter at the end,

462
00:19:04,540 --> 00:19:06,340
but were smarter at the beginning.

463
00:19:06,340 --> 00:19:08,420
Because of course, if you were smarter at the end,

464
00:19:08,420 --> 00:19:10,580
you might say, well, that's caused by the education.

465
00:19:10,580 --> 00:19:13,300
But this is where you get an early measure of intelligence,

466
00:19:13,300 --> 00:19:15,000
or at least when preceding the education,

467
00:19:15,000 --> 00:19:17,580
use it to predict educational attainment.

468
00:19:17,580 --> 00:19:22,260
And this is highly effective.

469
00:19:22,260 --> 00:19:27,180
So now let us do a regression on intelligence.

470
00:19:27,180 --> 00:19:29,540
First of all, say we all have an intuitive notion of what

471
00:19:29,540 --> 00:19:30,900
it means to be intelligent.

472
00:19:30,900 --> 00:19:33,580
So if you describe another human being as intelligent,

473
00:19:33,580 --> 00:19:36,220
most people don't say, well, what does that mean?

474
00:19:36,220 --> 00:19:37,220
What would that be like?

475
00:19:37,220 --> 00:19:38,740
I don't understand the word.

476
00:19:38,740 --> 00:19:40,980
The word makes no sense to me.

477
00:19:40,980 --> 00:19:42,740
So instead, normally, you know just

478
00:19:42,740 --> 00:19:45,260
what they're talking about, or at least in general terms,

479
00:19:45,260 --> 00:19:46,760
you know what they're talking about.

480
00:19:46,760 --> 00:19:49,060
So you have a basic ballpark idea.

481
00:19:49,060 --> 00:19:50,680
You might ask some follow-up questions,

482
00:19:50,680 --> 00:19:53,140
like are they like a kind of intelligent person

483
00:19:53,140 --> 00:19:55,380
who's really good at math, but nothing else?

484
00:19:55,380 --> 00:19:58,660
Or are they good at lots of things?

485
00:19:58,660 --> 00:20:18,100
And on the other hand, you all have an idea.


